# Potts, letter Sh - So

Shaddai.; Schaddai.

@AC 1992. "I am God Shaddai" (Genesis 17:1) in the sense of the letter, = the name of Abraham's god, by which name the Lord was first represented before them. Explained.

@--[2]. They had lost the name "Jehovah," and worshipped Shaddai... "I appeared unto Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob as god Shaddai" (Exodus 6:3).

@--[3]. Thus Abraham (at first) was an idolater, and had not as yet rejected the god Shaddai.

@--[4]. The reason the Lord was willing to be represented by the name "Shaddai" is that He never wants to suddenly destroy the worship implanted from infancy. Explained.

@--[5]. Translators render "Shaddai," "the Almighty;" others, "the Thunderer;" but it properly signifies "the Tempter," and "the Benefactor after temptations;" as is evident in Job. Illustrated.

@--. The word "shaddai" itself signifies vastation, thus temptation.

@--. As it originated from nations in Syria, He is not called "Elohim Shaddai," but "El Shaddai;" and in Job simply "Shaddai."

@--[6]. As there is comfort after temptations, they attributed the consequent good to the same Shaddai; and also the understanding of truth which is thence. Illustrated. And as He was thus held to be a god of truth - for vastation, temptation, chastisement, and rebuking are never of good - the name was retained in the Prophets, but by "Shaddai," there, is meant truth. Illustrated.

@--[7]. Isaac and Jacob also name the god Shaddai in a like sense: as one who tempts, and delivers from temptation, and afterwards benefits. Illustrated.

@--[8]. The worship of Shaddai originated from (their being) heard Spirits who reproved, and who afterwards comforted... They named the reproving Spirit Shaddai; and, as he afterwards comforted, the god Shaddai. Explained.

@2001. For the Lord is represented by the God Shaddai whom Abraham worshipped.

@3667. "God Shaddai will bless thee" (Genesis 28:3) = the temptations of that truth and good through which is conjunction. "God Shaddai" = temptations... because in ancient times they distinguished the supreme God, or the Lord, by various names, according to His attributes...

@--[3]. As to "God Shaddai," in special, the Lord had been so called in the Ancient Church relatively to temptations, and to blessings and benefits after them. Hence it is that by "God Shaddai," in the internal sense, are signified temptations.

@4162[2]. Those of the Ancient Church distinguished the Divine by various names... as "God Shaddai," from temptations, in which the Lord fights for man, and after which benefits him.

@4572. "I am God Shaddai" (Genesis 35:11) = the state of temptation past, and now Divine comfort; for "God Shaddai" = temptation and comfort afterwards...

@5376[6]. "Vastation from Shaddai" (Isaiah 13:6) = vastation in temptations.

@5628. "And God Shaddai" (Genesis 43:14) = consolation after hard things; for "Shaddai" = temptation and consolation after it.

@--. The Ancients distinguished the only God by various names... and as they believed temptations to be from Him, they called God, then, "Shaddai," and by this name understood not another god, but the only one as to temptations. But when the Ancient Church had declined, they began to worship as many gods as there had been names of the only God... each family having its own god... and the family of Terah worshipped Shaddai as theirs. @6003[end].

@--[2]. The reason not temptation only, but also consolation, is signified by "Shaddai," is that consolation follows all spiritual temptations.

@6429. "And with Shaddai" (Genesis 49:25) = the Lord the Benefactor after temptations. @AE 448[6].

@7193. "I appeared to Abraham, to Isaac, and to Jacob in God Shaddai" (Exodus 6:3) = the Lord's temptations as to the human, and the temptations of the faithful, and afterwards consolations.

@8764[4]. The finality of Divine truth in Heaven (is also signified by) "his voice as... the voice of Shaddai" (Ezekiel 1:24)... because "God Shaddai" = truth rebuking in temptations, and afterwards comforting. @AE 283[13].

@AE 283[4]. "Shaddai" (Psalms 68:14) = a state of temptations.

@386[16]. Temptations are signified by "the discipline of Shaddai" (Job 5:17).

@959[3]. "Shaddai," etc. are names of one God... but each = some attribute.

Shade.; Umbra.
Shady.; Umbrosus.
Shadowy.; Umbratilis.
Shader, A.; Umbraculum.
Shade, To.; Inumbrare.
Shadow forth.; Adumbrare.
Adumbration.; Adumbratio.
See <Evening>, <Night>, and <Obscure>.

@AC 1554[end]. Except slightly -umbratiliter-.

@1712. The shade in which were apparent goods and truths. Signified, Explained.

@--. It is called a state of shade when...

@--. The evil and falsity are what cause the shade -inumbrat-...

@1967. When people see anything in shade... these Spirits keep the mind fixed on the thought of some Thing...

@1972. (Heavenly objects) have sometimes appeared to me in the shade of the light of Heaven, which shade is not like the shade of mundane light; for it is light thinning out and weakening from inability to take it in.

@2367. "The shadow of my beam" (Genesis 19:8) = obscure general [perception]. Explained.

@2917. "Dead" = astate of shade or of night, that is, of ignorance.

@3341. Representations come forth by discriminations of light and shade... and all shade is from proprium; and from this (and the light from the Lord) are derived all the variegations of light and shade there.

@3384[2]. To "dwell in the shadow of death" (Isaiah 9:2) = in ignorance of good and truth.

@3438[2]. The things of the light of the world (are like) the shade of night. Man, being in this shade (believes) that his shade is light, and that light is shade. Explained.

@--. What appears in the internal sense, they believe to be of shade.

@3596. The illustration of (such things) brings them more into the shade.

@3693. So far as they are in shade, they are not in intelligence...

@3957[2]. When man dies, he passes from shade into light. Explained.

@3993[6]. Colours (there) are from variegations of light and shade.

@--. Shade there, although it appears as shade, is not like shade (here); for shade there is absence of light, consequently lack of intelligence...

@4018[2]. Things which enter without affection fall into the shade of the memory...

@4027[2]. Corporeal and worldly things... constantly throw shadows on the mind...

@4531[2]. It is this lumen which is called "the shadow of death." Explained.

@4644[2]. These things are only adumbrations.

@4715[5]. "The valley of the shadow" (Psalms 23:4) = lower things, which are relatively in the shade.

@4893. If explicated further, these things would fall into shade... For the understanding has its light and shade; and those things fall into its shade which do not coincide with those of which it previously had some notion. @4899.

@5084[2]. The fallacies of the senses... induce so great a shade on rational and spiritual things...

@5977[end]. They appear like shadows which flit about.

@6110[6]. The alternations (in Hell) are only variations of the shade and darkness of night.

@6400[3]. For all interior things are of shade and darkness to them.

@6723. "The land shadowing -adumbrans- with wings" (Isaiah 18:1) = the Church which darkens itself through reasonings from scientifics.

@8627. They call the knowledges of our Earth shadows.

@9213. That it must be restored before there is a state of shade from the delights of external loves. Signified, Explained.

@--. See <Set>.

@--. For when an Angel or Spirit is in externals, he is also in shade.

@9278[4]. When man is in externals... he is in such shade that...

@9433[2]. "For a shade in the day-time" (Isaiah 4:6). Explained.

@9642. Further from the interiors, truth is in shade.

@9723. The things which man does not love, he sees in the shade.

@10202. Elevation even... when truth is in its shade. Signified, Explained.

@10755. Something of shade from him appeared in the former place. Explained.

@HH 506. (Such) when let into their interiors, appear to themselves like those who come from shade into light.

@589[3]. The equilibrium... is as between light and shade... The light and shade operate nothing from themselves; but the heat through them... Falsity corresponds to shade... and spiritual shade is falsity.

@DSS 60. With these, the truth of the Word is as in the shade of night... If they see its shade, they falsify it.

@AR 110. The "shadow of death," etc. are nothing but the states of those in Hell, who are in falsities of evil.

@--[2]. "In the shadow of death" = the Gentiles, in good works, but not in truths.

@CL 79[6]. They said, We have seen them in a shade of light... We were seen in a shade, because the light of Heaven is a shade to them, and the shade of Hell is light...

@TCR 109[2]. The Israelitish worship shadowed forth -adumbraverit- the internal worship...

@--. Unless both the spiritual and the natural man are illustrated at the same time, the man is as in shade.

@671. Those washings figured and shadowed forth spiritual washings...

@SE 4102. Evil Spirits invent shadowy things -umbratilia-... and to these attach many other shadowy things, and phantasies...

@AE 298[5]. To be "a shade upon thy right hand" (Psalms 121:5) = to be a protection against evil and falsity; "a shade," here, is a sunshade -umbraculum-.

@481[3]. "The shadow of a cloud" (Isaiah 25:5) = a defence from these things. Explained.

@537[14]. "Through a land of dense shade" (Jeremiah 2:6) = a state of ignorance of truth and consequent Falsity. ( = the understanding of truth obscured. @730[38].)

@684[32]. To "live in His shadow" (Lamentations 4:20) = to be in His protection against the falsities of evil.

@706[16]. "The drawing back of the shadow on the steps of Ahaz" (Isaiah 38:8) = the prolongation of that time. @--.

@714[23]. The falsity in which these are is called "the shadow of death." (Psalms 44:19).

@727[2]. To "walk in a shady valley" (Psalms 23:4) = obscure understanding to which do not appear truths from light.

@1100[5]. To "dwell in the shadow of its branches" (Ezekiel 17:23) = terminated in natural truths; for these cover and guard rational truths...

@1216[3]. With these there is as it were a shader -umbraculum- between spiritual and natural light; yet with many this shader is transparent.

@LJPost 291. The defect and absence of (good and truth) correspond to shade (here).

@CanonsNC 17. Redeemer. 4. "The power of the Highest which should overshadow -adumbrabit-" = the Divine good.

