@Coro 38. (The Hell from the Most Ancient Church) lies in the middle southern quarter...

Sow.; Serere. Conserere.
See <Seed>.

@AC 1666[end]. Cities sown -conserere- with salt. Explained. @2455[end].

@2781[4]. "They who sow beside all waters" (Isaiah 32:20) = those who suffer themselves to be instructed in spiritual things.

@3941[3]. To "sow wheat" (Jeremiah 12:13) = the goods which are of love and charity.

@6154. To "sow" = to implant.

@9182[5]. "A land not sown" (Jeremiah 2:2) = defect of truths.

@9262[4]. "Neither ploughed nor sown" (Deuteronomy 21:4) = not cultivated with the truths and goods of faith, on account of ignorance.

@9272. "Six years thou shalt sow the land" (Exodus 23:10) = the first state, when the man of the Church is being instructed in the truths and goods of faith. Explained.

@--[3]. That to "sow the land," or "a field," = to teach and learn the truths and goods of faith which are of the Church. Illustrated.

@9274. "Not to sow the land" (in the seventh year) (Exodus 23:11) = not to be led by means of truths, as before.

@AE 374[9]. To "plough and sow" = to learn.

@386[21]. To "sow the fields" (Psalms 107:37) = to be instructed, and to receive truths.

@653[end]. "It is not sown -conseretur-" (Deuteronomy 29:23) = no receptibility of the truth of the Church.

@706[17]. To "sow and reap" = the implantation of good and its reception.

